I fixed it: a child run that stopped inside the night job's worktree can now be resumed from the main working tree with `exec run --register <id> --resume`, and it goes all the way through integration. A new regression test covers this scenario.

**Cause.** The child run's wait commit only goes into the job's branch. The exec branch picks it up when it syncs after the wait. The main working tree has neither the plan nor the waiting record, so resuming the reporter stage from there stopped with "plan not found". Copying the records over by hand puts the same files into main on a separate history, so they conflicted when the job branch was merged later.

**Fix.** Before resuming, the runner now:
- **Finds the wait record:** it takes the job-branch commit that holds the wait record (the second parent of the latest `exec(register <id>): merge <target> after wait` on the exec branch). If main's HEAD doesn't contain it, the runner merges that commit into main as `... records before resume`.
- **Leaves normal cases alone:** runs that stopped in main, and jobs that were already merged, are not touched.
- **Refuses cleanly on failure:** if the merge fails, it is aborted and the resume is refused. The output gives the cause and the next steps: resolve and resume again, resume inside the job worktree, or discard with `--force-restart`.
- **Gives a next step on other refusals:** when the plan or result can't be found, the message now points to `--force-restart` too.

**Choices I made:**
- **Why merge instead of copying:** main gets the records as history, so it shares the same commit with the job branch, and the later job merge has nothing to conflict on. Merging the exec branch's tip instead would make the integration stage wrongly think it was "already merged", so I take the commit that holds the wait record.
- **What comes along with it:** that commit also carries other items merged into the job branch earlier that night. Each passed its own post-review, but they reach main before the review of the job as a whole. The job's own output (run record, morning report) comes after the wait, so it is not brought in. The resume output shows how many commits came in. If you'd rather wait for the job review, merge the job first and then resume.
- **Not covered:** a child run already merged into the job branch with only worktree cleanup left. It has no post-wait merge, so nothing is brought in.

**Verification:**
- **New test:** inside a branch worktree standing in for the job, the test stops a child run, commits job output, resumes from main through to review and integration, and then merges the job branch with no conflict. With the fix switched off, the same test fails.
- **Existing tests:** the new test plus the existing ones in `register-resume.integration.test.ts` and `job-worktree.integration.test.ts`, 29 in all, passed.
- **Type check, ESLint and Prettier:** the type check and Prettier passed on the changed files. ESLint shows no errors and one warning: `resumeSingleRegisterItemWorktree` is now 395 lines against the 200-line guideline. It was already over the limit and this change made it longer. Splitting it is left for later.
- **History links:** they validate for the ticket.
- **Still to run:** `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint:ts`, `npm run test:unit`, `npm run lint:md` and the full integration suite are left to the parent runner.

I wrote up the cause, decisions and the CHANGELOG draft (bug fix) in the ticket, added a paragraph on resuming from main to the Job execution design (`sysd-job-execution.md`), and changed no protected settings. A leftover helper script, `tmp/fix-ticket.mjs`, is still there because deleting it needed approval. It sits under `tmp/`, which is not committed.

Changed files:
- `src/exec/register-foreign-wait.ts` (new)
- `src/exec/run/register-resume.ts`
- `tests/src/exec/register-resume.integration.test.ts`
- `docs/ja/product/040-system-design/sysd-job-execution.md`
- `docs/ja/projects/prj-0001/controls/project-register/pjr-97ke-night-child-run-resume.md`

